"waiting-memory-100","fresh","node",71.214],["waiting-memory-100","fresh","bun",49.077],["waiting-memory-100","fresh","go",21.553],["waiting-memory-100","persistent","nift",268.515,271.93,267.582,271.93,40.0,10.125,null,10.125,5],["waiting-memory-100","persistent","python",16.302,17.473,15.284,17.473,10.0,21.203,null,20.828,5],["waiting-memory-100","persistent","node",13.994,15.897,13.446,15.897,10.0,49.75,null,48.75,5],["waiting-memory-100","persistent","bun",22.399,23.618,21.543,23.618,20.0,23.625,null,23.0,5],["waiting-memory-100","persistent","go",13.344,16.548,12.096,16.548,0.0,3.0,null,3.0,5],["waiting-memory-1000","fresh","nift",2963.834],["waiting-memory-1000","fresh","python",126.353],["waiting-memory-1000","fresh","node",74.169],["waiting-memory-1000","fresh","bun",54.587],["waiting-memory-1000","fresh","go",26.465],["waiting-memory-1000","persistent","nift",2918.812,2966.206,2870.149,2966.206,900.0,143.75,null,143.5,5],["waiting-memory-1000","persistent","python",24.825,26.55,24.083,26.55,20.0,22.699,null,21.949,5],["waiting-memory-1000","persistent","node",20.536,23.316,18.566,23.316,30.0,53.125,null,50.125,5],["waiting-memory-1000","persistent","bun",24.402,26.054,22.732,26.054,20.0,27.625,null,26.0,5],["waiting-memory-1000","persistent","go",17.254,18.374,15.549,18.374,10.0,5.75,null,5.375,5],["waiting-memory-10000","fresh","python",242.076],["waiting-memory-10000","fresh","node",119.889],["waiting-memory-10000","fresh","bun",63.868],["waiting-memory-10000","fresh","go",69.911],["waiting-memory-10000","persistent","python",147.079,162.849,140.636,162.849,150.0,37.633,null,31.574,5],["waiting-memory-10000","persistent","node",56.996,79.877,54.621,79.877,90.0,74.211,null,55.0,5],["waiting-memory-10000","persistent","bun",30.971,36.173,29.289,36.173,40.0,37.125,null,29.375,5],["waiting-memory-10000","persistent","go",59.625,82.145,53.18,82.145,100.0,32.75,null,29.375,5]],"practitioner_html":"<section id=\"practitioner\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Qualitative practitioner assessment / not benchmark evidence</p><h2>Would I actually choose Nift for concurrent scripting?</h2><p>For bounded concurrent work in an existing Nift project, yes. For a general high-concurrency lightweight runtime or long-lived async service, not yet. Native threads and explicit sharing are real capabilities, but capture/registry growth, blocking waits and missing safer coordination APIs make the broader strength claim premature.</p><div class=\"table-scroll decision\"><table><thead><tr><th>Workload</th><th>Current preference</th><th>Why / boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Existing Nift project automation</th><td>Nift, bounded jobs</td><td>One .f policy, native joins, argv capture and explicit shared handles; avoid large retained task sets.</td></tr><tr><th>Async-heavy I/O tool/service</th><td>Node/Bun in JS; Go standalone</td><td>Suspended timers and cancellation/deadline APIs. Networking capacity is not measured here.</td></tr><tr><th>Parallel CPU work</th><td>Go; Python when its libraries are required</td><td>Absolute kernel throughput and pool costs matter alongside scaling. Native Nift threads do not remove interpreter cost.</td></tr><tr><th>Agent-maintained Nift automation</th><td>Nift for bounded .f workflows</td><td>Typed handles, snapshots and visible join/await are useful contracts; lock/error cleanup still needs care.</td></tr><tr><th>High-concurrency lightweight tasks</th><td>Go / Node / Bun</td><td>Nift task capture and completed-registry retention remain costly.</td></tr><tr><th>Existing JS / Python project</th><td>Its current runtime</td><td>Required async APIs or Python libraries, with explicit worker/process costs.</td></tr><tr><th>New standalone concurrent tool</th><td>Go</td><td>Buffered channels, context cancellation, race-debugging workflow and compiled deployment.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Useful explicitness survives the critique.</h3><p>Snapshot ordinary captures and typed transferable atomic/mutex handles expose sharing and ownership. A typed binding rejected substituting one handle kind for another. These are useful agent-visible semantics. Nested-index intermediates add no concurrency contract and are redundant ceremony. More lines are not automatically worse.</p><h3>Equal performance would change some choices.</h3><p>I would prefer Nift for existing project policy and bounded agent-maintained .f automation at equal reliable performance. I would still choose Go where channels, deadlines, cancellation and standalone deployment are requirements. Equal timing does not create missing APIs. A mature Nift with lightweight waits, bounded channels, guarded locks, group cancellation, pool controls and stable capture/registry ownership could earn a wider default role; no architectural rule requires another executor underneath.</p><p><strong>Product judgement: usable, with material caveats.</strong> Potentially compelling within its project runtime; not yet a demonstrated first-class strength across all concurrency classes. The separate core handover preserves minimal reproducers and profiles. No Nift core changed here.</p></section>"}
